lidation_feedback.json\\npostdraft_reference_query_plan_validation_feedback.md\\npostdraft_reference_retrieval.log\\npostdraft_references.bib\\npostdraft_references_provenance.json\\nreference_query_plan.json\\nreference_query_plan.md\\nreference_query_plan_agent_claude_20261111_075327.log\\nreference_query_plan_prompt.md\\nreference_query_plan_validation_feedback.json\\nreference_query_plan_validation_feedback.md\\nreference_retrieval.log\\nreferences.bib\\nreferences.openalex.bib\\nreferences.openalex.json\\nreferences.pre_postdraft.bib\\nreferences.pre_postdraft.json\\nreferences_provenance.json\\nresults\\nrevisions\\nscientific-evidence\\nscripts\\nsource_evidence\\nstrategy_validation_feedback.json\\nstrategy_validation_feedback.md\\ntask\\nvalidation_feedback.json\\nvalidation_feedback.md\\nvalidation_report.json\\n# Paper validation feedback\\n\\n- generated_at: 2026-11-11T08:45:34.414303+00:00\\n- paper_dir: `work/results/t06/claude/20261111_064241/paper`\\n- ok: `False`\\n\\n## Hard errors\\n- agentic evidence audit failed: see work/results/t06/claude/20261111_064241/paper/paper_evidence_audit_feedback.json and work/results/t06/claude/20261111_064241/paper/paper_evidence_audit.md\\n- figure validation failed: figures[2] figure_type does not match strategy for controls_permutation\\n\\n## Supplementary Information validation\\n- appendix_present: `True`\\n- heading_present: `True`\\n- placed_after_references: `True`\\n- pre_bibliography_clearpage_present: `True`\\n- clearpage_present: `True`\\n- visible_character_count: `7773`\\n- substantive_element_count: `10`\\n- rendered_checked: `False`\\n- rendered_heading_present: `False`\\n- rendered_visible_character_count: `0`\\n\\n## Evidence validation\\n- bundle_path: `work/results/t06/claude/20261111_064241/paper/scientific-evidence`\\n- bundle_present: `True`\\n- verification_report: `work/results/t06/claude/20261111_064241/paper/scientific-evidence/verification-report.json`\\n- total_references: `69`\\n- resolved_references: `23`\\n- anchored_references: `46`\\n- dangling_references: `0`\\n- manifests_checked: `6`\\n\\n## Agentic evidence audit\\n- checked: `True`\\n- verdict: `fail`\\n- finding_count: `3`\\n- summary: The held-out numbers and the qualification handling match the evidence trail, but the development sub-scores have no support in the grounding artifacts, one control is interpreted despite the paper's own no-claim statement, and \\\"verified\\\" wording contradicts the stated evaluator-opacity caveat.\\n\\n### Audit findings to fix\\n- `dev-subscores-ungrounded` (Results (Development tables), Table tab:dev, Supplement \\\"Development tables: additional detail\\\", Studied-vs-influence-only subsection): None of these sub-scores appears in any evidence-trail artifact. The paper itself says they are quoted from a log and have no registry entry. The claims_grounding entries cite result.best_correct_score and result.top_program_0x, which hold only the 0.4993 total. The derived inferences and the main-text B2 comparison on the development tables therefore rest on unregistered numbers.\\n  - claim: Configuration 1 splits into crowd 0.9987 and expert 0.0 with flip rate 0.5, random reference 0.0695, influence reference 0.4993, mean budget fraction 0.5007 and control gap 0.3153. From these the paper derives that neither MT-Bench pair flipped within the 68-row cap, that the crowd cases needed three rows, and that the development aggregate equals the influence-only reference.\\n  - evidence: paper_data.json: grep finds no 0.9987, 0.0695, 0.3153 or 0.5007; all_programs and top_programs carry only combined_score 0.4993478 and per-seed durations. claims_grounding.json claims 21-22 cite result.* ids that are scalar totals. The values appear only in strategy and memory prompt text (paper_strategy_prompt.md, global_memory_summary.md), not in the scientific-evidence trail. Verification report entries for these lines are only anchored to paper_data.json#all_programs and give a total score only.\\n  - requested_fix: Remove the unregistered sub-scores from Table tab:dev and the prose. Drop the derived statements about expert non-flip, three crowd rows, 0.5007 and the development influence-reference equality. Keep only the registered totals (0.4993 for all three configurations and the baseline, delta 0.0, 3 of 3 scored, runtimes). State that no per-family development breakdown is recorded.\\n- `copy-control-overinterpreted` (Results (Controls) vs Method (Comparators and validity controls)): Methods and the Supplement say the copy control's construction and thresholds are not documented and that no claim is made about it. Results then interprets it as ruling out a row-position artefact. That interpretation is unsupported and contradicts the paper's own caveat.\\n  - claim: The cross-table copy control flipped 0 of 2 trials, \\\"which is consistent with the budgets not being artefacts of an already-reversed pair or of row position.\\\"\\n  - evidence: claims_grounding.json claim \\\"Empty-set control flips no case; cross-table copy control flips 0 of 2 trials\\\" cites only final.evidence.episodes, which records copy_control {flips:0, trials:2} with no construction detail. paper.tex Method states \\\"we make no claim about them\\\". Supplement states \\\"its construction is not documented in the staged evidence\\\".\\n  - requested_fix: Keep the empty-set interpretation only if wanted (empty set flipped no case). For the copy control, report only \\\"0 flips in 2 trials\\\" and delete the \\\"row position\\\" inference.\\n- `verified-protocol-wording` (Introduction (Research question), Results Table tab:cases caption, Contributions): The paper repeatedly states that the evaluator source is not staged and that it could not inspect how the evaluator verifies a returned set. Calling the protocol or the budgets \\\"verified\\\" overstates the evidence. The only inspectable refit is the candidate's own.\\n  - claim: Budgets are measured \\\"by one verified protocol\\\", and $k$ is \\\"the verified number of removed rows\\\".\\n  - evidence: paper.tex Intro (\\\"we could not inspect how it verifies them\\\"), Method comparators section, Supplement \\\"Controls and qualification details\\\" (\\\"we could not inspect how the evaluator verifies it\\\"). claims_grounding.json: \\\"Evaluator source for comparators B1-B3 and the controls is not staged; only recorded outcomes ... are reported\\\".\\n  - requested_fix: Replace \\\"verified\\\" with \\\"evaluator-recorded\\\". For example, \\\"one evaluator-recorded protocol\\\" and \\\"the recorded number of removed rows\\\".\\n\\n## Soft warnings\\n- figures[0].font_sizes.title below minimum: 9.0 < 10.0\\n- figures[0].font_sizes.axis below minimum: 8.0 < 9.0\\n- figures[1].font_sizes.title below minimum: 9.5 < 10.0\\n- figures[1] PNG width too small: 2074 < 2400\\n- figures[2].font_sizes.title below minimum: 9.5 < 10.0\\n- figures[2] PNG width too small: 1835 < 2400\\n# Draft reference gap analysis\\n\\n**Status:** ready. Three targeted retrieval targets.\\n\\nCoverage of Bradley–Terry theory (extensions, existence/consistency, uncertainty) is dense. The existing bibliography does not cover three areas the draft relies on or motivates itself with. The draft concedes this in its \\\"Scope of the bibliography\\\" paragraph and in the Extended limitations. Numeric claims are grounded in `paper_data.json` and are not targets.\\n\\n## Missing literatures\\n- Chatbot Arena / MT-Bench pairwise LLM evaluation and the datasets used\\n- Influence functions and leave-one-out / Cook's-distance-style deletion diagnostics\\n- Manipulation and rigging of crowd-vote or pairwise-comparison rankings\\n\\n## Targets\\n\\n1. **arena-leaderboard-context** (Introduction, high, benchmark_context)\\n   - Anchor: \\\"Leaderboards that aggregate 'which of A and B is better' judgments into Bradley–Terry (BT) ratings are used to choose models…\\\"\\n   - Reason: The motivating claim about leaderboard use has no citation. The Data section uses Arena 55k/140k and MT-Bench human judgments without citing the platform or dataset papers.\\n   - Search topics: Chatbot Arena platform paper; MT-Bench human judgments / LLM-as-a-judge; Arena human-preference dataset.\\n\\n2. **influence-deletion-method** (Problem Setup and Method, high, background)\\n   - Anchor: \\\"Deleting row $i$ perturbs the stationarity condition by $-\\\\nabla\\\\ell_i$, so a Newton step predicts the change of the gap…\\\"\\n   - Reason: The first-order influence formula with leverage correction is the core of the studied configuration and the basis of the B2 comparator. It has no citation to influence-function or case-deletion literature. Only Broderick et al. is cited.\\n   - Search topics: influence functions for black-box predictions; Cook's distance / case deletion in logistic regression; one-step leave-one-out approximations for M-estimators.\\n\\n3. **manipulation-positioning** (Related Work, medium, positioning)\\n   - Anchor: \\\"Work on adversarial manipulation of crowd-vote leaderboards, on evaluation practices of arena-style platforms, and on influence functions…\\\"\\n   - Reason: The draft motivates the removal budget through deliberate action and rigging, then disclaims any positioning against that work. One or two citations would replace the disclaimer.\\n   - Search topics: adversarial vote manipulation of LLM arena leaderboards; robustness of pairwise-comparison rankings to corrupted comparisons.\\n\\n## Not targeted\\nRelated-work paragraphs on BT extensions, estimation and uncertainty are well supported. Adding more there would only grow the bibliography.\\npostdraft_citation_weave_agent_claude_20261111_075327.log\\npostdraft_citation_weave_prompt.md\\npostdraft_reference_query_plan.json\\npostdraft_reference_query_plan.md\\npostdraft_reference_query_plan_agent_claude_20261111_075327.log\\npostdraft_reference_query_plan_prompt.md\\npostdraft_reference_query_plan_validat