{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgvrgv9f9Qw7ce8Wj2peU","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_015cSThFRmkiaF6wqHQ2WWKt","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /workspace/paper; python3 - <<'E'\nimport re\nt=open('paper.tex').read()\nkeys=set()\nfor m in re.finditer(r'\\\\cite[a-z]*\\*?(?:\\[[^\\]]*\\])*\\{([^}]*)\\}',t):\n    keys|={k.strip() for k in m.group(1).split(',')}\nprint(len(keys),sorted(keys))\nb=set(re.findall(r'^@\\w+\\{([^,]+),',open('references.bib').read(),re.M))\nprint(len(b), keys-b)\nE\ngrep -n \"Scope of the bibliography\\|Chatbot Arena\\|influence function\" paper.tex | head -20"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":499,"cache_read_input_tokens":50018,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":499},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c05c501c-ede2-4806-b25e-2c46530a733f","uuid":"14872110-d87c-4ce6-89f1-0f21f24168a0","timestamp":"2026-11-11T08:53:03.596Z","request_id":"req_011CgvrgudfVNfcxaoyddLWT"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015cSThFRmkiaF6wqHQ2WWKt","type":"tool_result","content":"28 ['Bong2021GeneralizedRF', 'Broderick2020AnAF', 'Butler2004TheEO', 'Causeur2005A2E', 'Davidson1969MultivariatePC', 'Davidson1970OnET', 'Davidson1977OnET', 'Defresne2025PreferenceEF', 'Fageot2023GeneralizedBM', 'Gao2021UncertaintyQI', 'Genest1999DerivingPF', 'Hussain2013BayesianIO', 'Leonard1977AnAB', 'Li2021ThePO', 'Makur2024MinimaxHT', 'Morel-Balbi2025EstimationOP', 'Rizvi2026BayesianBF', 'Sawadogo2019CombiningMA', 'Selby2024PageRankAT', 'Simons1999AsymptoticsWT', 'Stern2011ModeratedPC', 'Varin2024TractableRR', 'Vojnović2019ConvergenceRO', 'Yan2011GROUPEDSP', 'Yan2025InferenceIA', 'https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.1703.04730', 'https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2403.04132', 'https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2501.17858']\n52 set()\n39:Public leaderboards built from pairwise comparisons fit a Bradley--Terry model and publish a ranking, yet how many comparisons must change before a ranking reverses is rarely measured. We study the smallest set of deleted comparisons, within a cap of 2\\% of all rows, that reverses the point-estimate order of one adjacent pair under a ridge-regularised Bradley--Terry refit, as recorded by the task's evaluator. We measure it on a crowd-vote table (Chatbot Arena, 135,630 comparisons) and an NBA game table (12,900 comparisons). Recorded budgets are small: 62 and 4 rows for the top and mid pairs of the crowd table (at most 0.046\\% of comparisons) and 62 and 8 rows for the sports table (0.48\\% and 0.062\\%). Mid-table pairs are cheaper than top pairs, and random deletion flips the top pairs in 0 of 20 draws. However, the evaluator's recorded influence-only reference (whose construction we could not verify) matched the studied configuration's budget in all six held-out cases, so no measured reduction relative to that reference is observed; the crowd-versus-sports contrast is confounded with table size, participants and era. No expert-judgment design was measured on held-out data, and the development stage records only aggregate scores, so we make no claim about expert annotation. All findings are descriptive: four cases, two designs, one rating model.\n43:Leaderboards that aggregate ``which of A and B is better'' judgments into Bradley--Terry (BT) ratings are used to choose models, to announce releases, and to compare research systems; Chatbot Arena is a prominent crowd-vote example \\citep{https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2403.04132}. The BT model and its many extensions are decades old \\citep{Davidson1970OnET,Davidson1969MultivariatePC,Leonard1977AnAB}, and the statistical properties of its estimates under sampling noise are increasingly well understood \\citep{Simons1999AsymptoticsWT,Gao2021UncertaintyQI,Li2021ThePO}. What is less often reported is a complementary, \\emph{combinatorial} question: how much of the underlying comparison table has to change, whether through noise or deliberate action, before a published rank order reverses?\n49:\\paragraph{What we do.} We measure recorded removal budgets on two held-out tables of real data: a deduplicated Chatbot Arena crowd-vote table and an NBA game-results table. We compare the studied search configuration, which orders rows by first-order influence on the rating gap and then refines the set with re-linearised greedy batches and a local polish, against three evaluator-owned comparators (random removal, first-order influence only, and opponent-targeted removal) and three validity controls (empty set, label permutation, cross-table copy). A separate pair of development tables, including a six-model expert-judgment table, is used to compare search configurations and is reported separately; only their aggregate scores are recorded.\n62:\\paragraph{Manipulation of crowd-vote leaderboards.} \\citet{https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2501.17858} study vote-rigging strategies against Chatbot Arena and their effect on model rankings. Our setting is a deletion-only perturbation with no adversary model, and we do not compare numbers with that work. Influence-based analysis of single training points is the tool behind our row ordering \\citep{https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.1703.04730}. Work on other evaluation practices of arena-style platforms, and on MT-Bench-style model-judge evaluation, is relevant background that is not in our reference set, and we make no claim of novelty relative to it.\n100:Table~\\ref{tab:data} lists the tables. The search procedure receives rows as integer participant indices with a coded outcome; names are not visible to it in the inspected candidate source. The held-out crowd table is the Chatbot Arena 140k preference release (data from the platform of \\citealp{https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2403.04132}) after content-hash deduplication against the development tables: from 135,634 raw rows, 2 were removed for content overlap with development data (a further 652 rows are listed in the evaluator metadata as prompt-only overlaps and were kept) and 2 rows of participants with fewer than 100 rows were dropped, leaving 135,630 rows over 52 models. The sports table holds NBA game results from 2006 to 2015 with 12,900 comparisons over 30 teams. The development tables are the Chatbot Arena 55k release (57,477 rows, 64 models) and the MT-Bench human-judgment table (3,355 rows, 6 models), the latter standing in for expert annotation. Dataset versions and licences are not recorded in the evidence we hold and are therefore not reported here. Model-judge data were not used.\n303:Four held-out cases on two designs, two identical episodes and twenty random draws are the entire basis for the held-out claims. The development side adds one expert table of six participants, for which no breakdown is recorded. We did not run exhaustive or exact optimisation on any table, so we cannot say how far the recorded budgets are from the minimum. We did not examine whether the reversed pairs are statistically distinguishable under sampling uncertainty, which would be the natural reading of an adjacent-pair flip for a practitioner. The bibliography available for this submission includes one work on vote rigging and one on influence functions but lacks the MT-Bench paper, classical case-deletion diagnostics and work on arena-platform practices beyond the platform description, so positioning relative to those lines is incomplete.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c05c501c-ede2-4806-b25e-2c46530a733f","uuid":"63a9dcc9-bb2d-4428-bdea-639be0c6366d","timestamp":"2026-11-11T08:53:03.646Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"28 ['Bong2021GeneralizedRF', 'Broderick2020AnAF', 'Butler2004TheEO', 'Causeur2005A2E', 'Davidson1969MultivariatePC', 'Davidson1970OnET', 'Davidson1977OnET', 'Defresne2025PreferenceEF', 'Fageot2023GeneralizedBM', 'Gao2021UncertaintyQI', 'Genest1999DerivingPF', 'Hussain2013BayesianIO', 'Leonard1977AnAB', 'Li2021ThePO', 'Makur2024MinimaxHT', 'Morel-Balbi2025EstimationOP', 'Rizvi2026BayesianBF', 'Sawadogo2019CombiningMA', 'Selby2024PageRankAT', 'Simons1999AsymptoticsWT', 'Stern2011ModeratedPC', 'Varin2024TractableRR', 'Vojnović2019ConvergenceRO', 'Yan2011GROUPEDSP', 'Yan2025InferenceIA', 'https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.1703.04730', 'https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2403.04132', 'https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2501.17858']\n52 set()\n39:Public leaderboards built from pairwise comparisons fit a Bradley--Terry model and publish a ranking, yet how many comparisons must change before a ranking reverses is rarely measured. We study the smallest set of deleted comparisons, within a cap of 2\\% of all rows, that reverses the point-estimate order of one adjacent pair under a ridge-regularised Bradley--Terry refit, as recorded by the task's evaluator. We measure it on a crowd-vote table (Chatbot Arena, 135,630 comparisons) and an NBA game table (12,900 comparisons). Recorded budgets are small: 62 and 4 rows for the top and mid pairs of the crowd t